1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,100
AFTER a week spent in professions of love and schemes of felicity, Mr. Collins was

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:13,566
called from his amiable Charlotte by the arrival of Saturday. The pain of separation,

3
00:00:13,566 --> 00:00:18,399
however, might be alleviated on his side by preparations for the reception of his bride,

4
00:00:18,399 --> 00:00:22,365
as he had reason to hope, that shortly after his next return into Hertfordshire,

5
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:27,765
the day would be fixed that was to make him the happiest of men. He took leave of his relations at

6
00:00:27,765 --> 00:00:32,799
Longbourn with as much solemnity as before; wished his fair cousins health and happiness again, and

7
00:00:32,799 --> 00:00:38,265
promised their father another letter of thanks. On the following Monday, Mrs. Bennet had the

8
00:00:38,265 --> 00:00:42,066
pleasure of receiving her brother and his wife, who came, as usual, to spend the Christmas

9
00:00:42,066 --> 00:00:47,632
at Longbourn. Mr. Gardiner was a sensible, gentlemanlike man, greatly superior to his sister,

10
00:00:47,632 --> 00:00:52,665
as well by nature as education. The Netherfield ladies would have had difficulty in believing

11
00:00:52,665 --> 00:00:57,966
that a man who lived by trade, and within view of his own warehouses, could have been so well-bred

12
00:00:57,966 --> 00:01:02,765
and agreeable. Mrs. Gardiner, who was several years younger than Mrs. Bennet and Mrs. Philips,

13
00:01:02,765 --> 00:01:07,966
was an amiable, intelligent, elegant woman, and a great favourite with her Longbourn nieces.

14
00:01:07,966 --> 00:01:13,365
Between the two eldest and herself especially, there subsisted a very particular regard. They

15
00:01:13,365 --> 00:01:17,765
had frequently been staying with her in town. The first part of Mrs. Gardiner’s business,

16
00:01:17,765 --> 00:01:23,165
on her arrival, was to distribute her presents and describe the newest fashions. When this was done,

17
00:01:23,165 --> 00:01:28,899
she had a less active part to play. It became her turn to listen. Mrs. Bennet had many grievances

18
00:01:28,899 --> 00:01:34,365
to relate, and much to complain of. They had all been very ill-used since she last saw her

19
00:01:34,365 --> 00:01:39,466
sister. Two of her girls had been on the point of marriage, and after all there was nothing in it.

20
00:01:39,466 --> 00:01:46,132
“I do not blame Jane,” she continued, “for Jane would have got Mr. Bingley if she could. But,

21
00:01:46,132 --> 00:01:52,132
Lizzy! Oh, sister! it is very hard to think that she might have been Mr. Collins’s wife by this

22
00:01:52,132 --> 00:01:58,200
time, had not it been for her own perverseness. He made her an offer in this very room, and she

23
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:03,833
refused him. The consequence of it is, that Lady Lucas will have a daughter married before I have,

24
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:09,399
and that Longbourn estate is just as much entailed as ever. The Lucases are very artful people,

25
00:02:09,399 --> 00:02:14,032
indeed, sister. They are all for what they can get. I am sorry to say it of them,

26
00:02:14,032 --> 00:02:19,365
but so it is. It makes me very nervous and poorly, to be thwarted so in my own family, and

27
00:02:19,365 --> 00:02:24,299
to have neighbours who think of themselves before anybody else. However, your coming just at this

28
00:02:24,299 --> 00:02:29,032
time is the greatest of comforts, and I am very glad to hear what you tell us of long sleeves.”

29
00:02:29,865 --> 00:02:33,165
Mrs. Gardiner, to whom the chief of this news had been given before,

30
00:02:33,165 --> 00:02:37,066
in the course of Jane and Elizabeth’s correspondence with her, made her sister

31
00:02:37,066 --> 00:02:41,399
a slight answer, and, in compassion to her nieces, turned the conversation.

32
00:02:42,299 --> 00:02:47,799
When alone with Elizabeth afterwards, she spoke more on the subject. “It seems likely to have

33
00:02:47,799 --> 00:02:53,500
been a desirable match for Jane,” said she. “I am sorry it went off. But these things happen

34
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:58,966
so often! A young man, such as you describe Mr. Bingley, so easily falls in love with a pretty

35
00:02:58,966 --> 00:03:04,500
girl for a few weeks, and, when accident separates them, so easily forgets her, that these sort of

36
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:11,566
“An excellent consolation in its way,” said Elizabeth; “but it will not do for

37
00:03:11,566 --> 00:03:16,265
us. We do not suffer by accident. It does not often happen that the interference of

38
00:03:16,265 --> 00:03:20,632
friends will persuade a young man of independent fortune to think no more of a girl whom he was

39
00:03:20,632 --> 00:03:26,265
violently in love with only a few days before.” “But that expression of ‘violently in love’ is

40
00:03:26,265 --> 00:03:33,066
so hackneyed, so doubtful, so indefinite, that it gives me very little idea. It is as often applied

41
00:03:33,066 --> 00:03:37,433
to feelings which arise only from a half hour’s acquaintance, as to a real, strong attachment.

42
00:03:37,433 --> 00:03:43,500
Pray, how violent was Mr. Bingley’s love?” “I never saw a more promising inclination;

43
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:49,299
he was growing quite inattentive to other people, and wholly engrossed by her. Every time they met,

44
00:03:49,299 --> 00:03:53,865
it was more decided and remarkable. At his own ball he offended two or three

45
00:03:53,865 --> 00:03:58,665
young ladies by not asking them to dance; and I spoke to him twice myself without receiving an

46
00:03:58,665 --> 00:04:04,265
answer. Could there be finer symptoms? Is not general incivility the very essence of love?”

47
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:11,632
“Oh, yes! of that kind of love which I suppose him to have felt. Poor Jane! I am sorry for her,

48
00:04:11,632 --> 00:04:16,265
because, with her disposition, she may not get over it immediately. It had better have happened

49
00:04:16,265 --> 00:04:21,600
to you, Lizzy; you would have laughed yourself out of it sooner. But do you think she would be

50
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:27,100
prevailed on to go back with us? Change of scene might be of service—and perhaps a little relief

51
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:31,865
from home may be as useful as anything.” Elizabeth was exceedingly pleased with

52
00:04:31,865 --> 00:04:35,333
this proposal, and felt persuaded of her sister’s ready acquiescence.

53
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:41,399
“I hope,” added Mrs. Gardiner, “that no consideration with regard to this young man

54
00:04:41,399 --> 00:04:46,899
will influence her. We live in so different a part of town, all our connections are so different,

55
00:04:46,899 --> 00:04:51,365
and, as you well know, we go out so little, that it is very improbable they should meet

56
00:04:51,365 --> 00:04:57,532
at all, unless he really comes to see her.” “And that is quite impossible; for he is now in

57
00:04:57,532 --> 00:05:02,632
the custody of his friend, and Mr. Darcy would no more suffer him to call on Jane in such a part of

58
00:05:02,632 --> 00:05:08,333
London! My dear aunt, how could you think of it? Mr. Darcy may, perhaps, have heard of such a place

59
00:05:08,333 --> 00:05:13,299
as Gracechurch Street, but he would hardly think a month’s ablution enough to cleanse him from its

60
00:05:13,299 --> 00:05:18,600
impurities, were he once to enter it; and, depend upon it, Mr. Bingley never stirs without him.”

61
00:05:19,566 --> 00:05:24,899
“So much the better. I hope they will not meet at all. But does not Jane correspond with his

62
00:05:24,899 --> 00:05:30,532
sister? She will not be able to help calling.” “She will drop the acquaintance entirely.”

63
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:35,100
But, in spite of the certainty in which Elizabeth affected to place this point,

64
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:39,233
as well as the still more interesting one of Bingley’s being withheld from seeing Jane,

65
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:43,733
she felt a solicitude on the subject which convinced her, on examination, that she

66
00:05:43,733 --> 00:05:49,365
did not consider it entirely hopeless. It was possible, and sometimes she thought it probable,

67
00:05:49,365 --> 00:05:55,200
that his affection might be re-animated, and the influence of his friends successfully combated by

68
00:05:55,200 --> 00:06:00,600
the more natural influence of Jane’s attractions. Miss Bennet accepted her aunt’s invitation with

69
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:05,265
pleasure; and the Bingleys were no otherwise in her thoughts at the same time than as she hoped,

70
00:06:05,265 --> 00:06:07,600
by Caroline’s not living in the same house with her brother,

71
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:11,933
she might occasionally spend a morning with her, without any danger of seeing him.

72
00:06:12,665 --> 00:06:17,500
The Gardiners stayed a week at Longbourn; and what with the Philipses, the Lucases, and the officers,

73
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:22,500
there was not a day without its engagement. Mrs. Bennet had so carefully provided for the

74
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:27,600
entertainment of her brother and sister, that they did not once sit down to a family dinner. When the

75
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:32,265
engagement was for home, some of the officers always made part of it, of which officers Mr.

76
00:06:32,265 --> 00:06:38,100
Wickham was sure to be one; and on these occasions Mrs. Gardiner, rendered suspicious by Elizabeth’s

77
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:43,433
warm commendation of him, narrowly observed them both. Without supposing them, from what she saw,

78
00:06:43,433 --> 00:06:48,233
to be very seriously in love, their preference of each other was plain enough to make her a little

79
00:06:48,233 --> 00:06:52,500
uneasy; and she resolved to speak to Elizabeth on the subject before she left Hertfordshire,

80
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:56,165
and represent to her the imprudence of encouraging such an attachment.

81
00:06:56,933 --> 00:07:01,665
To Mrs. Gardiner, Wickham had one means of affording pleasure, unconnected with his

82
00:07:01,665 --> 00:07:07,265
general powers. About ten or a dozen years ago, before her marriage, she had spent a considerable

83
00:07:07,265 --> 00:07:11,399
time in that very part of Derbyshire to which he belonged. They had, therefore,

84
00:07:11,399 --> 00:07:16,000
many acquaintance in common; and, though Wickham had been little there since the death of Darcy’s

85
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,966
father, five years before, it was yet in his power to give her fresher intelligence of her former

86
00:07:19,966 --> 00:07:25,600
friends than she had been in the way of procuring. Mrs. Gardiner had seen Pemberley, and known the

87
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:31,000
late Mr. Darcy by character perfectly well. Here, consequently, was an inexhaustible subject of

88
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,399
discourse. In comparing her recollection of Pemberley with the minute description which

89
00:07:35,399 --> 00:07:40,000
Wickham could give, and in bestowing her tribute of praise on the character of its late possessor,

90
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,066
she was delighting both him and herself. On being made acquainted with the present Mr.

91
00:07:45,066 --> 00:07:49,299
Darcy’s treatment of him, she tried to remember something of that gentleman’s reputed disposition,

92
00:07:49,299 --> 00:07:54,066
when quite a lad, which might agree with it; and was confident, at last, that she recollected

93
00:07:54,066 --> 00:07:59,566
having heard Mr. Fitzwilliam Darcy formerly spoken of as a very proud, ill-natured boy.

94
00:07:59,566 --> 00:08:02,233
“Will you come and see me.”

95
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:03,299
CHAPTER XXVI.

96
00:08:10,433 --> 00:08:15,299
MRS. GARDINER’S caution to Elizabeth was punctually and kindly given on the first

97
00:08:15,299 --> 00:08:17,399
favourable opportunity of speaking to her alone:

98
00:08:17,399 --> 00:08:21,233
after honestly telling her what she thought, she thus went on:—

99
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,466
“You are too sensible a girl, Lizzy, to fall in love merely because you are warned against it;

100
00:08:26,466 --> 00:08:31,733
and, therefore, I am not afraid of speaking openly. Seriously, I would have you be on

101
00:08:31,733 --> 00:08:36,066
your guard. Do not involve yourself, or endeavour to involve him, in an affection

102
00:08:36,066 --> 00:08:40,966
which the want of fortune would make so very imprudent. I have nothing to say against him:

103
00:08:40,966 --> 00:08:46,200
he is a most interesting young man; and if he had the fortune he ought to have, I should think you

104
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:51,466
could not do better. But as it is—you must not let your fancy run away with you. You have sense,

105
00:08:51,466 --> 00:08:56,700
and we all expect you to use it. Your father would depend on your resolution and good conduct,

106
00:08:56,700 --> 00:09:03,532
I am sure. You must not disappoint your father.” “My dear aunt, this is being serious indeed.”

107
00:09:04,066 --> 00:09:07,299
“Yes, and I hope to engage you to be serious likewise.”

108
00:09:08,165 --> 00:09:12,966
“Well, then, you need not be under any alarm. I will take care of myself,

109
00:09:12,966 --> 00:09:17,700
and of Mr. Wickham too. He shall not be in love with me, if I can prevent it.”

110
00:09:18,466 --> 00:09:24,966
“Elizabeth, you are not serious now.” “I beg your pardon. I will try again. At present

111
00:09:24,966 --> 00:09:30,466
I am not in love with Mr. Wickham; no, I certainly am not. But he is, beyond all comparison, the most

112
00:09:30,466 --> 00:09:36,000
agreeable man I ever saw—and if he becomes really attached to me—I believe it will be better that

113
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:43,000
he should not. I see the imprudence of it. Oh, that abominable Mr. Darcy! My father’s opinion

114
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,865
of me does me the greatest honour; and I should be miserable to forfeit it. My father, however,

115
00:09:47,865 --> 00:09:52,799
is partial to Mr. Wickham. In short, my dear aunt, I should be very sorry to be the means of making

116
00:09:52,799 --> 00:09:58,066
any of you unhappy; but since we see, every day, that where there is affection young people are

117
00:09:58,066 --> 00:10:02,165
seldom withheld, by immediate want of fortune, from entering into engagements with each other,

118
00:10:02,165 --> 00:10:06,966
how can I promise to be wiser than so many of my fellow-creatures, if I am tempted,

119
00:10:06,966 --> 00:10:11,632
or how am I even to know that it would be wiser to resist? All that I can promise you,

120
00:10:11,632 --> 00:10:16,665
therefore, is not to be in a hurry. I will not be in a hurry to believe myself his first

121
00:10:16,665 --> 00:10:22,265
object. When I am in company with him, I will not be wishing. In short, I will do my best.”

122
00:10:23,333 --> 00:10:28,200
“Perhaps it will be as well if you discourage his coming here so very often. At least you

123
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:34,299
should not remind your mother of inviting him.” “As I did the other day,” said Elizabeth, with

124
00:10:34,299 --> 00:10:40,365
a conscious smile; “very true, it will be wise in me to refrain from that. But do not imagine that

125
00:10:40,365 --> 00:10:44,632
he is always here so often. It is on your account that he has been so frequently invited this week.

126
00:10:44,632 --> 00:10:50,399
You know my mother’s ideas as to the necessity of constant company for her friends. But really,

127
00:10:50,399 --> 00:10:55,433
and upon my honour, I will try to do what I think to be wisest; and now I hope you are satisfied.”

128
00:10:56,466 --> 00:11:01,433
Her aunt assured her that she was; and Elizabeth, having thanked her for the kindness of her hints,

129
00:11:01,433 --> 00:11:07,132
they parted,—a wonderful instance of advice being given on such a point without being resented.

130
00:11:07,132 --> 00:11:12,399
Mr. Collins returned into Hertfordshire soon after it had been quitted by the Gardiners and Jane;

131
00:11:12,399 --> 00:11:14,765
but, as he took up his abode with the Lucases,

132
00:11:14,765 --> 00:11:19,899
his arrival was no great inconvenience to Mrs. Bennet. His marriage was now fast approaching;

133
00:11:19,899 --> 00:11:25,433
and she was at length so far resigned as to think it inevitable, and even repeatedly to say, in an

134
00:11:25,433 --> 00:11:31,132
ill-natured tone, that she “wished they might be happy.” Thursday was to be the wedding-day,

135
00:11:31,132 --> 00:11:36,165
and on Wednesday Miss Lucas paid her farewell visit; and when she rose to take leave, Elizabeth,

136
00:11:36,165 --> 00:11:40,966
ashamed of her mother’s ungracious and reluctant good wishes, and sincerely affected herself,

137
00:11:40,966 --> 00:11:45,066
accompanied her out of the room. As they went down stairs together, Charlotte said,—

138
00:11:45,665 --> 00:11:48,532
“I shall depend on hearing from you very often, Eliza.”

139
00:11:49,066 --> 00:11:52,733
“That you certainly shall.” “And I have another favour

140
00:11:52,733 --> 00:11:58,066
to ask. Will you come and see me?” “We shall often meet, I hope, in Hertfordshire.”

141
00:11:58,799 --> 00:12:03,966
“I am not likely to leave Kent for some time. Promise me, therefore, to come to Hunsford.”

142
00:12:04,665 --> 00:12:08,765
Elizabeth could not refuse, though she foresaw little pleasure in the visit.

143
00:12:09,532 --> 00:12:14,399
“My father and Maria are to come to me in March,” added Charlotte, “and I hope you

144
00:12:14,399 --> 00:12:19,433
will consent to be of the party. Indeed, Eliza, you will be as welcome to me as either of them.”

145
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:24,532
The wedding took place: the bride and bridegroom set off for Kent from the church door,

146
00:12:24,532 --> 00:12:30,700
and everybody had as much to say or to hear on the subject as usual. Elizabeth soon heard from her

147
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:35,566
friend, and their correspondence was as regular and frequent as it ever had been: that it should

148
00:12:35,566 --> 00:12:40,799
be equally unreserved was impossible. Elizabeth could never address her without feeling that all

149
00:12:40,799 --> 00:12:45,532
the comfort of intimacy was over; and, though determined not to slacken as a correspondent,

150
00:12:45,532 --> 00:12:51,365
it was for the sake of what had been rather than what was. Charlotte’s first letters were received

151
00:12:51,365 --> 00:12:55,799
with a good deal of eagerness: there could not but be curiosity to know how she would speak of her

152
00:12:55,799 --> 00:13:00,600
new home, how she would like Lady Catherine, and how happy she would dare pronounce herself to be;

153
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:05,765
though, when the letters were read, Elizabeth felt that Charlotte expressed herself on every point

154
00:13:05,765 --> 00:13:10,966
exactly as she might have foreseen. She wrote cheerfully, seemed surrounded with comforts,

155
00:13:10,966 --> 00:13:15,665
and mentioned nothing which she could not praise. The house, furniture, neighbourhood,

156
00:13:15,665 --> 00:13:20,032
and roads, were all to her taste, and Lady Catherine’s behaviour was most friendly and

157
00:13:20,032 --> 00:13:24,899
obliging. It was Mr. Collins’s picture of Hunsford and Rosings rationally softened;

158
00:13:24,899 --> 00:13:28,600
and Elizabeth perceived that she must wait for her own visit there, to know the rest.

159
00:13:29,566 --> 00:13:33,765
Jane had already written a few lines to her sister, to announce their safe arrival in London;

160
00:13:33,765 --> 00:13:38,265
and when she wrote again, Elizabeth hoped it would be in her power to say something of the Bingleys.

161
00:13:38,265 --> 00:13:44,165
Her impatience for this second letter was as well rewarded as impatience generally is. Jane had been

162
00:13:44,165 --> 00:13:48,899
a week in town, without either seeing or hearing from Caroline. She accounted for it, however,

163
00:13:48,899 --> 00:13:53,799
by supposing that her last letter to her friend from Longbourn had by some accident been lost.

164
00:13:54,532 --> 00:13:59,865
“My aunt,” she continued, “is going to-morrow into that part of the town, and I shall take the

165
00:13:59,865 --> 00:14:04,299
opportunity of calling in Grosvenor Street.” She wrote again when the visit was paid,

166
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:09,600
and she had seen Miss Bingley. “I did not think Caroline in spirits,” were her words,

167
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:14,165
“but she was very glad to see me, and reproached me for giving her no notice of my coming to

168
00:14:14,165 --> 00:14:19,600
London. I was right, therefore; my last letter had never reached her. I inquired after their brother,

169
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:25,299
of course. He was well, but so much engaged with Mr. Darcy that they scarcely ever saw him.

170
00:14:25,299 --> 00:14:31,265
I found that Miss Darcy was expected to dinner: I wish I could see her. My visit was not long,

171
00:14:31,265 --> 00:14:35,933
as Caroline and Mrs. Hurst were going out. I dare say I shall soon see them here.”

172
00:14:36,665 --> 00:14:41,333
Elizabeth shook her head over this letter. It convinced her that accident only could discover

173
00:14:41,333 --> 00:14:47,500
to Mr. Bingley her sister’s being in town. Four weeks passed away, and Jane saw nothing

174
00:14:47,500 --> 00:14:52,132
of him. She endeavoured to persuade herself that she did not regret it; but she could no longer

175
00:14:52,132 --> 00:14:57,665
be blind to Miss Bingley’s inattention. After waiting at home every morning for a fortnight, and

176
00:14:57,665 --> 00:15:03,066
inventing every evening a fresh excuse for her, the visitor did at last appear; but the shortness

177
00:15:03,066 --> 00:15:07,966
of her stay, and, yet more, the alteration of her manner, would allow Jane to deceive herself

178
00:15:07,966 --> 00:15:13,000
no longer. The letter which she wrote on this occasion to her sister will prove what she felt:—

179
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:18,700
“My dearest Lizzy will, I am sure, be incapable of triumphing in her better judgment, at my expense,

180
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:23,100
when I confess myself to have been entirely deceived in Miss Bingley’s regard for me.

181
00:15:23,632 --> 00:15:29,566
But, my dear sister, though the event has proved you right, do not think me obstinate if I still

182
00:15:29,566 --> 00:15:35,566
assert that, considering what her behaviour was, my confidence was as natural as your suspicion.

183
00:15:35,566 --> 00:15:39,532
I do not at all comprehend her reason for wishing to be intimate with me; but,

184
00:15:39,532 --> 00:15:44,632
if the same circumstances were to happen again, I am sure I should be deceived again. Caroline

185
00:15:44,632 --> 00:15:49,966
did not return my visit till yesterday; and not a note, not a line, did I receive in the meantime.

186
00:15:49,966 --> 00:15:55,365
When she did come, it was very evident that she had no pleasure in it; she made a slight,

187
00:15:55,365 --> 00:16:00,600
formal apology for not calling before, said not a word of wishing to see me again, and was,

188
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:05,799
in every respect, so altered a creature, that when she went away I was perfectly resolved to continue

189
00:16:05,799 --> 00:16:11,933
the acquaintance no longer. I pity, though I cannot help blaming, her. She was very wrong

190
00:16:11,933 --> 00:16:17,632
in singling me out as she did; I can safely say, that every advance to intimacy began on her side.

191
00:16:18,165 --> 00:16:22,865
But I pity her, because she must feel that she has been acting wrong, and because I am

192
00:16:22,865 --> 00:16:27,899
very sure that anxiety for her brother is the cause of it. I need not explain myself farther;

193
00:16:27,899 --> 00:16:32,500
and though we know this anxiety to be quite needless, yet if she feels it,

194
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:37,899
it will easily account for her behaviour to me; and so deservedly dear as he is to his sister,

195
00:16:37,899 --> 00:16:43,899
whatever anxiety she may feel on his behalf is natural and amiable. I cannot but wonder, however,

196
00:16:43,899 --> 00:16:48,700
at her having any such fears now, because if he had at all cared about me, we must have

197
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:53,865
met long, long ago. He knows of my being in town, I am certain, from something she said herself;

198
00:16:53,865 --> 00:16:59,032
and yet it would seem, by her manner of talking, as if she wanted to persuade herself that he is

199
00:16:59,032 --> 00:17:04,865
really partial to Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of judging harshly,

200
00:17:04,865 --> 00:17:09,532
I should be almost tempted to say, that there is a strong appearance of duplicity in all this.

201
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:15,466
I will endeavour to banish every painful thought, and think only of what will make me happy, your

202
00:17:15,466 --> 00:17:21,365
affection, and the invariable kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Let me hear from you very soon.

203
00:17:21,365 --> 00:17:25,865
Miss Bingley said something of his never returning to Netherfield again, of giving up the house,

204
00:17:25,865 --> 00:17:31,566
but not with any certainty. We had better not mention it. I am extremely glad that you have such

205
00:17:31,566 --> 00:17:36,365
pleasant accounts from our friends at Hunsford. Pray go to see them, with Sir William and Maria.

206
00:17:36,365 --> 00:17:39,899
I am sure you will be very comfortable there. “Yours, etc.”

207
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:45,100
This letter gave Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits returned, as she considered that Jane

208
00:17:45,100 --> 00:17:49,733
would no longer be duped, by the sister at least. All expectation from the brother was

209
00:17:49,733 --> 00:17:55,566
now absolutely over. She would not even wish for any renewal of his attentions. His character sunk

210
00:17:55,566 --> 00:18:00,066
on every review of it; and, as a punishment for him, as well as a possible advantage to Jane,

211
00:18:00,066 --> 00:18:04,365
she seriously hoped he might really soon marry Mr. Darcy’s sister, as,

212
00:18:04,365 --> 00:18:08,032
by Wickham’s account, she would make him abundantly regret what he had thrown away.

213
00:18:09,233 --> 00:18:13,600
Mrs. Gardiner about this time reminded Elizabeth of her promise concerning that gentleman,

214
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:18,700
and required information; and Elizabeth had such to send as might rather give contentment to her

215
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:23,933
aunt than to herself. His apparent partiality had subsided, his attentions were over,

216
00:18:23,933 --> 00:18:29,200
he was the admirer of some one else. Elizabeth was watchful enough to see it all, but she

217
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:34,066
could see it and write of it without material pain. Her heart had been but slightly touched,

218
00:18:34,066 --> 00:18:37,865
and her vanity was satisfied with believing that she would have been his only choice,

219
00:18:37,865 --> 00:18:43,365
had fortune permitted it. The sudden acquisition of ten thousand pounds was the most remarkable

220
00:18:43,365 --> 00:18:47,333
charm of the young lady to whom he was now rendering himself agreeable; but Elizabeth,

221
00:18:47,333 --> 00:18:51,899
less clear-sighted perhaps in this case than in Charlotte’s, did not quarrel with

222
00:18:51,899 --> 00:18:56,632
him for his wish of independence. Nothing, on the contrary, could be more natural; and,

223
00:18:56,632 --> 00:19:00,532
while able to suppose that it cost him a few struggles to relinquish her, she was ready

224
00:19:00,532 --> 00:19:05,165
to allow it a wise and desirable measure for both, and could very sincerely wish him happy.

225
00:19:05,865 --> 00:19:11,165
All this was acknowledged to Mrs. Gardiner; and, after relating the circumstances, she thus went

226
00:19:11,165 --> 00:19:16,365
on:—“I am now convinced, my dear aunt, that I have never been much in love; for had I really

227
00:19:16,365 --> 00:19:21,966
experienced that pure and elevating passion, I should at present detest his very name, and wish

228
00:19:21,966 --> 00:19:27,399
him all manner of evil. But my feelings are not only cordial towards him, they are even impartial

229
00:19:27,399 --> 00:19:32,700
towards Miss King. I cannot find out that I hate her at all, or that I am in the least unwilling

230
00:19:32,700 --> 00:19:37,865
to think her a very good sort of girl. There can be no love in all this. My watchfulness has been

231
00:19:37,865 --> 00:19:42,000
effectual; and though I should certainly be a more interesting object to all my acquaintance,

232
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,865
were I distractedly in love with him, I cannot say that I regret my comparative insignificance.

233
00:19:46,865 --> 00:19:53,265
Importance may sometimes be purchased too dearly. Kitty and Lydia take his defection much more to

234
00:19:53,265 --> 00:19:58,299
heart than I do. They are young in the ways of the world, and not yet open to the mortifying

235
00:19:58,299 --> 00:20:02,933
conviction that handsome young men must have something to live on as well as the plain.”

236
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:05,700
CHAPTER XXVII.

237
00:20:14,165 --> 00:20:19,600
WITH no greater events than these in the Longbourn family, and otherwise diversified by little beyond

238
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:25,299
the walks to Meryton, sometimes dirty and sometimes cold, did January and February pass

239
00:20:25,299 --> 00:20:31,733
away. March was to take Elizabeth to Hunsford. She had not at first thought very seriously of

240
00:20:31,733 --> 00:20:36,899
going thither; but Charlotte, she soon found, was depending on the plan, and she gradually

241
00:20:36,899 --> 00:20:42,165
learned to consider it herself with greater pleasure as well as greater certainty. Absence

242
00:20:42,165 --> 00:20:46,665
had increased her desire of seeing Charlotte again, and weakened her disgust of Mr. Collins.

243
00:20:46,665 --> 00:20:52,365
There was novelty in the scheme; and as, with such a mother and such uncompanionable sisters,

244
00:20:52,365 --> 00:20:58,365
home could not be faultless, a little change was not unwelcome for its own sake. The journey would,

245
00:20:58,365 --> 00:21:03,233
moreover, give her a peep at Jane; and, in short, as the time drew near, she would have been very

246
00:21:03,233 --> 00:21:08,933
sorry for any delay. Everything, however, went on smoothly, and was finally settled according to

247
00:21:08,933 --> 00:21:14,566
Charlotte’s first sketch. She was to accompany Sir William and his second daughter. The improvement

248
00:21:14,566 --> 00:21:19,566
of spending a night in London was added in time, and the plan became as perfect as plan could be.

249
00:21:20,399 --> 00:21:24,466
The only pain was in leaving her father, who would certainly miss her, and who,

250
00:21:24,466 --> 00:21:26,933
when it came to the point, so little liked her going,

251
00:21:26,933 --> 00:21:30,600
that he told her to write to him, and almost promised to answer her letter.

252
00:21:31,600 --> 00:21:36,233
The farewell between herself and Mr. Wickham was perfectly friendly; on his side even more.

253
00:21:36,233 --> 00:21:40,566
His present pursuit could not make him forget that Elizabeth had been the first to excite

254
00:21:40,566 --> 00:21:45,399
and to deserve his attention, the first to listen and to pity, the first to be admired;

255
00:21:45,399 --> 00:21:50,466
and in his manner of bidding her adieu, wishing her every enjoyment, reminding her of what she

256
00:21:50,466 --> 00:21:55,000
was to expect in Lady Catherine de Bourgh, and trusting their opinion of her—their opinion of

257
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:00,532
everybody—would always coincide, there was a solicitude, an interest, which she felt

258
00:22:00,532 --> 00:22:05,933
must ever attach her to him with a most sincere regard; and she parted from him convinced, that,

259
00:22:05,933 --> 00:22:11,466
whether married or single, he must always be her model of the amiable and pleasing.

260
00:22:11,466 --> 00:22:15,399
Her fellow-travellers the next day were not of a kind to make her think him less

261
00:22:15,399 --> 00:22:19,600
agreeable. Sir William Lucas, and his daughter Maria, a good-humoured girl,

262
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:24,532
but as empty-headed as himself, had nothing to say that could be worth hearing, and were listened

263
00:22:24,532 --> 00:22:28,966
to with about as much delight as the rattle of the chaise. Elizabeth loved absurdities,

264
00:22:28,966 --> 00:22:33,233
but she had known Sir William’s too long. He could tell her nothing new of the wonders

265
00:22:33,233 --> 00:22:37,500
of his presentation and knighthood; and his civilities were worn out, like his information.

266
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:43,200
It was a journey of only twenty-four miles, and they began it so early as to be in Gracechurch

267
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:48,600
Street by noon. As they drove to Mr. Gardiner’s door, Jane was at a drawing-room window watching

268
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:52,500
their arrival: when they entered the passage, she was there to welcome them, and Elizabeth,

269
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:57,899
looking earnestly in her face, was pleased to see it healthful and lovely as ever. On the

270
00:22:57,899 --> 00:23:01,966
stairs were a troop of little boys and girls, whose eagerness for their cousin’s appearance

271
00:23:01,966 --> 00:23:06,399
would not allow them to wait in the drawing-room, and whose shyness, as they had not seen her for a

272
00:23:06,399 --> 00:23:12,000
twelvemonth, prevented their coming lower. All was joy and kindness. The day passed

273
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:17,265
most pleasantly away; the morning in bustle and shopping, and the evening at one of the theatres.

274
00:23:17,265 --> 00:23:21,665
Elizabeth then contrived to sit by her aunt. Their first subject was her sister;

275
00:23:21,665 --> 00:23:26,200
and she was more grieved than astonished to hear, in reply to her minute inquiries,

276
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:30,299
that though Jane always struggled to support her spirits, there were periods

277
00:23:30,299 --> 00:23:35,399
of dejection. It was reasonable, however, to hope that they would not continue long. Mrs.

278
00:23:35,399 --> 00:23:39,365
Gardiner gave her the particulars also of Miss Bingley’s visit in Gracechurch Street,

279
00:23:39,365 --> 00:23:43,600
and repeated conversations occurring at different times between Jane and herself,

280
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:47,933
which proved that the former had, from her heart, given up the acquaintance.

281
00:23:48,532 --> 00:23:51,600
Mrs. Gardiner then rallied her niece on Wickham’s desertion,

282
00:23:51,600 --> 00:23:57,066
and complimented her on bearing it so well. “But, my dear Elizabeth,” she added,

283
00:23:57,066 --> 00:24:01,865
“what sort of girl is Miss King? I should be sorry to think our friend mercenary.”

284
00:24:02,466 --> 00:24:07,066
“Pray, my dear aunt, what is the difference in matrimonial affairs, between the mercenary

285
00:24:07,066 --> 00:24:13,299
and the prudent motive? Where does discretion end, and avarice begin? Last Christmas you were

286
00:24:13,299 --> 00:24:17,333
afraid of his marrying me, because it would be imprudent; and now, because he is trying

287
00:24:17,333 --> 00:24:21,966
to get a girl with only ten thousand pounds, you want to find out that he is mercenary.”

288
00:24:22,665 --> 00:24:27,532
“If you will only tell me what sort of girl Miss King is, I shall know what to think.”

289
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:31,665
“She is a very good kind of girl, I believe. I know no harm of her.”

290
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:35,566
“But he paid her not the smallest attention till her grandfather’s

291
00:24:35,566 --> 00:24:41,200
death made her mistress of this fortune?” “No—why should he? If it were not allowable

292
00:24:41,200 --> 00:24:45,100
for him to gain my affections, because I had no money, what occasion could there

293
00:24:45,100 --> 00:24:48,833
be for making love to a girl whom he did not care about, and who was equally poor?”

294
00:24:49,733 --> 00:24:54,365
“But there seems indelicacy in directing his attentions towards her so soon after this event.”

295
00:24:55,132 --> 00:25:00,000
“A man in distressed circumstances has not time for all those elegant decorums

296
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,665
which other people may observe. If she does not object to it, why should we?”

297
00:25:04,566 --> 00:25:09,100
“Her not objecting does not justify him. It only shows her being deficient

298
00:25:09,100 --> 00:25:15,665
in something herself—sense or feeling.” “Well,” cried Elizabeth, “have it as you choose.

299
00:25:15,665 --> 00:25:22,365
He shall be mercenary, and she shall be foolish.” “No, Lizzy, that is what I do not choose. I should

300
00:25:22,365 --> 00:25:25,966
be sorry, you know, to think ill of a young man who has lived so long in Derbyshire.”

301
00:25:26,632 --> 00:25:32,165
“Oh, if that is all, I have a very poor opinion of young men who live in Derbyshire; and their

302
00:25:32,165 --> 00:25:37,500
intimate friends who live in Hertfordshire are not much better. I am sick of them all. Thank

303
00:25:37,500 --> 00:25:42,000
heaven! I am going to-morrow where I shall find a man who has not one agreeable quality, who has

304
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:47,466
neither manners nor sense to recommend him. Stupid men are the only ones worth knowing, after all.”

305
00:25:48,233 --> 00:25:52,500
“Take care, Lizzy; that speech savours strongly of disappointment.”

306
00:25:53,632 --> 00:25:58,066
Before they were separated by the conclusion of the play, she had the unexpected happiness

307
00:25:58,066 --> 00:26:01,966
of an invitation to accompany her uncle and aunt in a tour of pleasure which they

308
00:26:01,966 --> 00:26:05,333
proposed taking in the summer. “We have not quite determined

309
00:26:05,333 --> 00:26:09,665
how far it shall carry us,” said Mrs. Gardiner; “but perhaps, to the Lakes.”

310
00:26:10,299 --> 00:26:14,333
No scheme could have been more agreeable to Elizabeth, and her acceptance of the

311
00:26:14,333 --> 00:26:20,799
invitation was most ready and grateful. “My dear, dear aunt,” she rapturously cried, “what

312
00:26:20,799 --> 00:26:27,833
delight! what felicity! You give me fresh life and vigour. Adieu to disappointment and spleen. What

313
00:26:27,833 --> 00:26:34,365
are men to rocks and mountains? Oh, what hours of transport we shall spend! And when we do return,

314
00:26:34,365 --> 00:26:37,865
it shall not be like other travellers, without being able to give one accurate

315
00:26:37,865 --> 00:26:43,665
idea of anything. We will know where we have gone—we will recollect what we have seen. Lakes,

316
00:26:43,665 --> 00:26:48,299
mountains, and rivers, shall not be jumbled together in our imaginations; nor, when we

317
00:26:48,299 --> 00:26:52,733
attempt to describe any particular scene, will we begin quarrelling about its relative situation.

318
00:26:52,733 --> 00:26:58,365
Let our first effusions be less insupportable than those of the generality of travellers.”

319
00:27:00,765 --> 00:27:01,000
CHAPTER XXVIII.
